%[text] %[text:anchor:T_288EC79D] # Internal Flow
%[text] [⇦ Return to the main menu](file:../MainMenu.m)
%[text] Internal flow is crucial for engineering as it involves the study of fluid in an enclosed system, such as pipes and ducts. Understanding internal flow is necessary for designing an efficient and effective transportation, ventilation, and heating system. In [PressureVelocity.m](file:./PressureVelocity.m) we introduced Bernoulli's equation for ideal flow. In this script, we will learn how to model internal fluid flow using Bernoulli's equation for non-ideal flow, including pressure loss due to friction.
%[text] 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Cover image house plumbing](text:image:184a)
%[text]{"align":"center"} 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![decorative](text:image:1ee4)
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"} %[text:anchor:M_597CA885]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4C8EC99A] ## **Before you get started**
%[text] This Live Script is intended to be used with the code visible and output inline. On the **View** tab of the MATLAB Toolstrip, in the **View** section, select **Output Inline**. Alternately, select **Output inline** using the icon ![Live Script Output Inline](text:image:8ec6) at the top right of the Live Editor pane.
%[text] ![Lightbulb mark](text:image:8858) Interacting with this Live Script will build some familiarity with MATLAB as concepts and commands are introduced together. If you need more instruction, consider taking [MATLAB Onramp](https://matlabacademy.mathworks.com/details/matlab-onramp/gettingstarted), a free 2-hour online tutorial that teaches the essentials of MATLAB.
%[text] ![Warning symbol](text:image:7a30)   For an optimal experience, follow the instructions and steps in the given sequence. Proceed to a new section only after completing the preceding one. Some sections depend on variables created in prior sections and will generate errors if run out of order.
%[text] The ![Try this icon](text:image:6d09)  and  ![Exercise icon](text:image:36cc) icons refer to two different types of interactive activities that you will find in this script. The ![Try this icon](text:image:1e98)  usually indicates an interaction where you will explore the visualization of some concept introduced in this script. The ![Exercise icon](text:image:89f7) interactions are designed to challenge your understanding of those concepts and may be used for grading and completion checks by your professor.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_FBD061C4] ## Ideal vs. non-ideal internal flow
%[text] In [PressureVelocity.m](file:./PressureVelocity.m), we introduced Bernoulli's equation:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{P}{\\rho g} + \\frac{v^2}{2 g} + z = H\_0=\\text{Constant}\n\\qquad\n(\\text{Head-loss form})${"altText":"\frac{P}{\rho g} + \frac{v^2}{2 g} + z = H\_0=\text{Constant} \qquad (\text{Head-loss form})"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $P+\n\\frac{1}{2}\\rho v^2+\n\\rho g z= \\text{Constant} \n\\qquad\n(\\text{Pressure form})${"altText":"P+ \frac{1}{2}\rho v^2+ \rho g z= \text{Constant}  \qquad (\text{Pressure form})"}
%[text] This relation states that the fluid’s total pressure or energy is conserved along a streamline. This is an idealistic view of fluid flow, and particularly for internal flow, the energy needs to be conserved. Take the example of a house with its plumbing; the energy of the water flowing through the pipe is not entirely conserved from when it enters the house through the main water intake to when it leaves the house to the sewer system. For example, in every pipe, the friction of water against the pipe wall dissipates energy and reduces the pressure. Moreover, every elbow, change in diameter, valve, opening, etc., is also associated with energy dissipation. This dissipation of energy is usually referred to as head loss.
%[text]{"align":"center"} 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F3D2BE5B] ## How to estimate head loss
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4BE8E087] ### Pipe head loss
%[text] 
%[text] The head losses due to friction in a pipe are usually defined using the Darcy-Weisbach equation:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $H\_l = f\_D \\frac{L}{D}\\frac{V^2}{2g}\n\\qquad\n(\\text{Head-loss form})${"altText":"H\_l = f\_D \frac{L}{D}\frac{V^2}{2g} \qquad (\text{Head-loss form})"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\Delta P = \\rho g H\_l\n\\qquad\n(\\text{Pressure form})${"altText":"\Delta P = \rho g H\_l \qquad (\text{Pressure form})"}
%[text] Where $L${"altText":"L"} is the pipe length, $D${"altText":"D"} its diameter, $V${"altText":"V"} the velocity of the flow, and $g${"altText":"g"} is the local acceleration due to gravity. The coefficient $f\_D${"altText":"f\_D"} is Darcy's friction factor. This friction factor has been studied and tabulated experimentally. It is usually expressed as a function of the pipe material and the flow regime. Generally, the material is defined through a relative roughness ratio $\\eta=\\frac{\\epsilon}{D}${"altText":"\eta=\frac{\epsilon}{D}"}, where $\\epsilon${"altText":"\epsilon"} is the roughness of the pipe (in units of length) and $D${"altText":"D"} is the diameter of the pipe. The Reynolds number characterizes the regime of the flow:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\text{Re} = \\frac{\\rho VD}{\\mu}${"altText":"\text{Re} = \frac{\rho VD}{\mu}"}
%[text] The Reynolds number is a common dimensionless number used in fluid dynamics. Generally, a high Reynolds number indicates turbulent flow, while a low Reynolds number indicates a laminar regime. There are [multiple formulas](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Darcy_friction_factor_formulae) to calculate the friction coefficient as a function of the Reynolds number and the material roughness. Each formula is more or less accurate depending on the range of roughness and the regime. 
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_BC084111] #### Laminar regime
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Pond outlow animated image (laminar regime)](text:image:22fe)
%[text]{"align":"center"} Pond outflow
%[text]{"align":"center"} 
%[text] The head loss in a pipe in a laminar regime was determined analytically by Poiseuille:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $f\_D = \\frac{64}{\\text{Re}}\n\\qquad\n\\text{with Re} \< 3000${"altText":"f\_D = \frac{64}{\text{Re}} \qquad \text{with Re} \< 3000"}
%[text] The Reynolds number, Re, is a non-dimensional number that helps characterize the flow regime. A Reynolds number under 3000 corresponds to the laminar regime for pipe flow.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\text{Re} = \\frac{\\rho VD}{\\mu}${"altText":"\text{Re} = \frac{\rho VD}{\mu}"}
%[text] Where $\\rho${"altText":"\rho"} is the density of the fluid, $V${"altText":"V"} its velocity, and $\\mu${"altText":"\mu"} the dynamic viscosity. $D${"altText":"D"} a length scale that depends on the problem considered, commonly the diameter of pipe flow.
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_91ED3598] #### Turbulent regime
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Animated image of Sabbaday fall in New Hampshire (turbulent flow)](text:image:13ce)
%[text]{"align":"center"} Sabbaday Falls, New Hampshire
%[text]{"align":"center"} 
%[text] The head loss in a pipe in a turbulent regime can be determined using the Colebrook-White equation:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{f\_d}} = -2\\log\\left(\\frac{\\epsilon}{3.7D\_H}+\\frac{2.51}{\\text{Re}\\sqrt{f\_D}} \\right)\n\\qquad\n\\text{with Re} \> 4000${"altText":"\frac{1}{\sqrt{f\_d}} = -2\log\left(\frac{\epsilon}{3.7D\_H}+\frac{2.51}{\text{Re}\sqrt{f\_D}} \right) \qquad \text{with Re} \> 4000"}
%[text] In this equation, $\\frac{\\epsilon}{D\_H}${"altText":"\frac{\epsilon}{D\_H}"} corresponds to the relative roughness of the pipe, $\\epsilon${"altText":"\epsilon"} corresponds to the roughness, and is expressed in units of length (usually mm or inches). The Colebrook-White equation is non-linear and is solved using an iterative process.
%[text] 
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:3244) **Try.** Find Darcy's friction coefficient, $f\_D = f(\\text{Re},\\epsilon)${"altText":"f\_D = f(\text{Re},\epsilon)"}, using the Colebrook-White equation.
fd = FrictionColebrook(4000, 0.001) %[control:slider:6efd]{"position":[24,28]} %[control:slider:8782]{"position":[30,35]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:14ba) **Exercise.** Follow the instructions on the plot to complete this exercise.
  MoodyPlotExercise %[control:button:1bff]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B4B3AB5D] ### Minor head loss
%[text] 
%[text] Major head losses occur in the pipe due to friction against the pipe wall. Minor head losses are the head losses caused by the disturbance of the flow. Common sources of minor head loss include fittings such as elbows, restrictions, orifices, and valves. When designing a fluid network, the fittings manufacturer can usually provide tabulated data of the head losses as a function of the fitting’s parameter and the flow characteristics (Reynolds number). 
%[text] In this script, we will use tabulated data from the Simscape Fluids library, valid for standard piping equipment and can be adjusted to more particular settings. For example, the [documentation page](https://www.mathworks.com/help/hydro/ref/elbowtl.html) of the Elbow (TL) turn in the thermal liquid library provides a table of the friction factor as a function of the nominal size of the elbow.
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7AFA618B] ## Application to house plumbing
%[text] 
%[text] A common fluid network is the plumbing system in a house. In this section, we will model simple house plumbing, considering a shower on both house floors and a washer on the second floor.
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_EF2AE482] ### Head losses identification
%[text] 
%[text] The first step in modeling any fluid network is identifying the present head losses.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Illustration of house plumbing](text:image:6c36)
%[text]{"align":"center"} 
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:66cc) **Exercise.** Click the figure below to identify all the head losses.
  %[control:button:790c]{"position":[1,2]}
IdentifyHeadLoss
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_FAA10188] ### First model
%[text] 
%[text] In this section, we are considering a simplified plumbing version that does not include the washer and only some of the fittings provided in the previous figures.
%[text] 
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:31e7) **Exercise.** Intuitively, which shower head should have the highest flow rate?
 CheckMdl1QFlowRate(0) %[control:dropdown:54af]{"position":[21,22]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:4b0f) **Try.** Open the model and follow the instructions provided.
open("House_Mdl1.slx")
  %[control:button:77f5]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_2A929111] ### Equalizing the network
%[text] 
%[text] We are trying to ensure that both showers can provide the same temperature and mass flow. 
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:41d0) **Exercise.** What could you do to make sure both showers flow the same way?
CheckMdl1Solution(0) %[control:dropdown:4a2e]{"position":[19,20]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:6249) **Try.** Open the second model and follow the instructions provided.
open("House_Mdl2.slx")
  %[control:button:14b2]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6046A67C] ### Adding the washer on the network
%[text] 
%[text] In this section, we are adding the washer onto the piping network. When the washer is not running, the flow through both shower heads should be unchanged. However, we want to verify the shower stays comfortable when the washer turns on.
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:02d0) **Exercise.** Intuitively, how would the washer influence the mass flow through the showers?
  %[control:button:65b0]{"position":[1,2]}
CheckMassFlowWasher(0,0) %[control:dropdown:7878]{"position":[21,22]} %[control:dropdown:750f]{"position":[23,24]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:6d74) **Exercise.** Intuitively, how would the washer influence the temperature through the showers?
  %[control:button:0046]{"position":[1,2]}
CheckTemperatureWasher(0,0) %[control:dropdown:9b2f]{"position":[24,25]} %[control:dropdown:2bb5]{"position":[26,27]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:9c69) **Try.** Open and run the third model.
open("House_Mdl3.slx")
  %[control:button:7715]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] You can explore the results from this from the simulation in the Simscape Results app:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Screenshot showing the location of the Simscape results app in the Simulink toolstrip](text:image:15c4)
%[text] Explore the results of the first and second-floor showers and plot the temperature as a function of time.
%%
%[text] Both showers are greatly affected when the washer starts its washing cycle, so much so that it makes the shower temperature dangerous.
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:6f18) **Exercise.** What could we do to remediate this situation?
CheckRemediation(0) %[control:dropdown:6a2f]{"position":[18,19]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:276d) **Try.** Open and run the fourth model.
open("House_Mdl4.slx")
  %[control:button:674c]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E8E65B58] ## Further exploration
%[text] 
%[text] This script should have helped you to develop some practical intuition of how pipe flow works and how to equilibrate a network. The best way to gain more understanding is by practicing. The model presented in this script can be modified to try different scenarios. You could try things like this:
%[text] - Make the network more complex by adding more fixtures, such as a sink and toilet.
%[text] - Play with the dimensions of the pipe. \
%[text] 
%[text] [⇦ Return to the main menu](file:../MainMenu.m)
